{
  "id": "80815",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Acid–Base Properties of Salt Solutions",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "___selfClosing___": true,
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "uzqwdfmm15eyrax",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4eka4yj536aidvd",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "When an acid and base are mixed, they undergo a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "neutralization reaction"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A reaction between stoichiometrically equivalent amounts of an acid and a base; a reaction between an acid and a base that produces a salt and water."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1787149660",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1963402926",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". The word "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "neutralization"
              },
              {
                "text": " seems to imply that a stoichiometrically equivalent solution of an acid and a base would be "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "neutral"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Describes a solution in which [H"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "3"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "O"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "+"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "] = [OH"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "−"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "]."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4156080031",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "338598424",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". This is sometimes true, but the salts that are formed in these reactions may have acidic or basic properties of their own, as we shall now see."
              }
            ],
            "id": "mbekel94oaafdr4",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A solution is neutral when it contains equal concentrations of hydronium [H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "] and hydroxide [OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "] ions. When we mix solutions of an acid and a base, an acid–base neutralization reaction occurs. However, even if we mix stoichiometrically equivalent quantities, we may find that the resulting solution is not neutral. It could contain either an excess of hydronium ions or an excess of hydroxide ions because the nature of the salt formed determines whether the solution is acidic, neutral, or basic. The following four situations illustrate how solutions with various pH values can arise following a neutralization reaction using stoichiometrically equivalent quantities:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "x11qsrybb8vidxb",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "A strong acid and a strong base"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ", such as HCl("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "aq"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ") and NaOH("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "aq"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "), will react to form a "
                      },
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "neutral solution"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " because the conjugate partners produced are of negligible strength."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4286428557",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "hplwrj2liogyrrz",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "A strong acid and a weak base"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " yields a "
                      },
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "weakly acidic solution"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ". The products of this neutralization reaction are the conjugate base of the strong acid ("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "b"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " ≈ 0, so it doesn’t affect pH) and the conjugate acid of the weak base ("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "a"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " > 0, so it ionizes to make the solution acidic)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2800202438",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "nv8ku3wz03a8pw5",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "A weak acid and a strong base"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " yields a "
                      },
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "weakly basic solution"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ". The products of this neutralization reaction are the conjugate acid of the strong base ("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "a"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " ≈ 0, so it doesn’t affect pH) and the conjugate base of the weak acid ("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "b"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " > 0, so it ionizes to make the solution basic)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "168920897",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "toabjyopdci3mjz",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "A weak acid plus a weak base"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " can yield either an acidic or a basic solution. This is the most complex of the four types of reactions. When the "
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "conjugate acid"
                          }
                        ],
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "children": [
                              {
                                "text": "Substance formed when a base gains a proton."
                              }
                            ],
                            "id": "3899366791",
                            "type": "p"
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "258474878",
                        "trigger": "hover",
                        "type": "popup"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " and the "
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "conjugate base"
                          }
                        ],
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "children": [
                              {
                                "text": "Substance formed when an acid loses a proton."
                              }
                            ],
                            "id": "3638457266",
                            "type": "p"
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "3568724140",
                        "trigger": "hover",
                        "type": "popup"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " are of unequal strengths, the solution can be either acidic or basic, depending on the relative strengths of the two conjugates. To predict whether a particular combination will be acidic or basic, tabulated "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " values of the conjugates must be compared. (Note: Occasionally, the weak acid and the weak base can have the same strength, so their respective conjugate base and acid will have the same strength, and the solution will be neutral.)"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1503367409",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "42p22k0l3pkvp7s",
                "type": "li"
              }
            ],
            "id": "sx1w59jgsj31yxp",
            "style": "decimal",
            "type": "ol"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "g8ki8q322bzr7zd",
                "text": "Ion Effect on pH"
              }
            ],
            "id": "a9jh6r6g782ptdn",
            "type": "h2"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Another way to look at the acid−base properties of a salt solution is to determine how the cation and the anion of the salt react with water and affect pH. Anions of strong acids (NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", Cl"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", Br"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ") react negligibly with water and therefore do not affect the pH of the solution. Anions of weak acids (CN"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", F"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ") react with water to form OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", thereby increasing the pH of a solution. For example, a salt with CN"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " as an anion, when dissolved in water, will react with water to produce the weak acid HCN and OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ut5yd1idnu3h1g9",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "gzl6t0k14dw5fy2",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "r2h2ekfj56l8skx",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{CN}^-\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm{HCN}\\;+\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Similarly, cations of strong bases (alkali metals and heavier alkaline earth metals) will not react with water and do not affect pH. Cations that are conjugate acids of weak bases such as NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " react with water to form the weak base and H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", thereby decreasing the pH of the solution."
              }
            ],
            "id": "5qguxfqqgmze5qh",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "l03pm3gb0z0chqt",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "0wm2n1od00g3vf3",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{NH}}_4^+\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm{NH}}_3\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For an "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "amphiprotic"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Describes species that may either donate or accept a proton."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "429530379",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1736217055",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " anion, such as HCO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", a comparison of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "a"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "b"
              },
              {
                "text": " is required to determine whether its presence will increase of decrease pH. If "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "a"
              },
              {
                "text": " > "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "b"
              },
              {
                "text": ", pH will be decreased, and if "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "b"
              },
              {
                "text": " > K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "a"
              },
              {
                "text": ", pH will be increased."
              }
            ],
            "id": "rwlnpxxv0nb0qfk",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "1719224310",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Determine whether aqueous solutions of the following salts are acidic, basic, or neutral:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "bdeag03u8bw07cz",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "KBr"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ux02m4cnpoelqpb",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " cation and the Br"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " anion are both "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "spectator ions"
                      }
                    ],
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "Ions that are unchanged by a reaction but present in the reaction mixture. They appear in overall ionic equations but not in net ionic equations."
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "3973432690",
                        "type": "p"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "774977000",
                    "trigger": "hover",
                    "type": "popup"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", since they are the cation of a strong base (KOH) and the anion of a strong acid (HBr), respectively. The solution is neutral."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "rdejsti7uw0xdpk",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "kyy6nt220y16lqw",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ion is acidic and the Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ion is a spectator. The solution will be acidic."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "5tv2h90s52pp0kp",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "NaHCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "vzewxu9ij0xfs4a",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " The Na"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " cation is a cation of a strong base and will not affect the pH of the solution, while the HCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " anion is amphiprotic. The "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " of HCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is 4.7 × 10"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−11"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", and its "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "b"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " ",
                        "type": "text"
                      }
                    ],
                    "src": "<m:math display=\"inline\"> <m:mrow> <m:mfrac> <m:mrow> <m:mn>1.0</m:mn> <m:mo lspace=\"2px\" rspace=\"2px\">&times;</m:mo> <m:msup> <m:mn>10</m:mn> <m:mrow> <m:mo>&minus;</m:mo> <m:mn>14</m:mn> </m:mrow> </m:msup> </m:mrow> <m:mrow> <m:mn>4.3</m:mn> <m:mo lspace=\"2px\" rspace=\"2px\">&times;</m:mo> <m:msup> <m:mn>10</m:mn> <m:mrow> <m:mo>&minus;</m:mo> <m:mn>7</m:mn></m:math>",
                    "subtype": "mathml",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 2.3 × 10"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−8"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". Since "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "b"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " >> "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", the solution is basic."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "5jh5snntu6zehnq",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "F"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "x84i5toxbd53pmh",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ion is listed as being acidic, and the F"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ion is listed as a base, so we must directly compare the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " of the two ions. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " of NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is 5.6 × 10"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−10"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", which seems very small, yet the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "b"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " of F"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is 1.4 × 10"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−11"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", so the solution is acidic, since "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " > "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "b"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "0nsgxow4r0wxbhl",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
